as King of Pergamon from 159 to 138 BCE, succeeding his brother Eumenes II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attalus-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attalus III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attalus III was the last Attalid king of Pergamon, reigning from 138 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attambelos-vii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attambelos-vii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attambelos VII</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attambelos VII was king of Characene, the trading kingdom at the head of the Persian Gulf, in the early 2nd century CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attar-shumki-i"><span class="enc-name">Attar-shumki I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attar-shumki I was king of Arpad, the Aramaean state of Bit-Agusi in northern Syria, around 800 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-attarine-madrasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-attarine-madrasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Attarine Madrasa</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The al-Attarine Madrasa is a college for Islamic studies in Fez, Morocco, next to the Qarawiyyin Mosque, built from 1323 to 1325 by the Marinid sultan Abu Said Uthman II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attarissiya"><span class="enc-name">Attarissiya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attarissiya was a warlord described in Hittite records as 'the man of Ahhiya' around 1400 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attic-standard"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attic-standard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attic standard</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Attic standard was the weight standard for silver coinage originating in Athens, based on a tetradrachm of about 17 grams.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attica"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attica</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Attica is the peninsula of east-central Greece that formed the territory of ancient Athens.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attigny"><span class="enc-name">Attigny</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Attigny is a small town on the Aisne in the Ardennes department of north-eastern France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attila"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attila</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attila was ruler of the Huns from 434, at first jointly with his brother Bleda, and sole ruler from about 445 until his death in 453.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attimabbe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attimabbe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attimabbe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attimabbe was a Jain noblewoman of the Western Chalukya court in the late tenth and early eleventh centuries, the widow of the general Nagadeva.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/attock"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/attock.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Attock</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Attock is a town in Punjab, Pakistan, on the Indus near its junction with the Kabul River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attrition"><span class="enc-name">Attrition</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Attrition refers to a form of warfare in which victory depends on wearing down the enemy's manpower and material resources through continuous losses.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/attus-clausus"><span class="enc-name">Attus Clausus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Attus Clausus was a Sabine leader who, according to Roman tradition, moved to Rome around 504 BCE with a large band of followers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/atuatuca"><span class="enc-name">Atuatuca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Atuatuca was a stronghold in the territory of the Eburones in northern Gaul, described by Julius Caesar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/au-viet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/au-viet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Âu Việt</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Âu Việt were an ancient people of the northern uplands of present-day Vietnam and southern China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auch"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auch.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auch</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Auch is a town in Gascony, south-western France, and the capital of the Gers department.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aucilla-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aucilla-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aucilla River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aucilla River flows about 140 kilometres from southern Georgia across northern Florida to the Gulf of Mexico.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auckland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auckland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auckland</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Auckland is the largest city of New Zealand, on an isthmus between two harbours in the north of the North Island.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aude-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aude-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aude (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aude is a river in southern France, about 224 km long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/audiencia"><span class="enc-name">Audiencia</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">An audiencia was a high court of justice in the Spanish Empire, which in the Americas and the Philippines also exercised administrative and political functions.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/audoleon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/audoleon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Audoleon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Audoleon was king of Paeonia from about 315 to 285 BCE, son of King Patraos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aughnanure-castle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aughnanure-castle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aughnanure Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Aughnanure Castle is a six-storey tower house near Oughterard, County Galway, Ireland, built by the O'Flaherty lords of Iar Connacht in the 15th or 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augsburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augsburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augsburg</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augsburg is a city in Bavaria, southern Germany, and was a free imperial city of the Holy Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augsburg-confession.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augsburg Confession</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Augsburg Confession is the founding statement of Lutheran belief, drafted by Philip Melanchthon and presented to Emperor Charles V at the Diet of Augsburg in 1530.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-hermann-francke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-hermann-francke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August Hermann Francke</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">August Hermann Francke (1663–1727) was a German Lutheran clergyman, theologian and educator, a leader of Pietism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Revolution · 1945</span><span class="enc-snip">The August Revolution was the seizure of power across Vietnam by the Viet Minh in August 1945, after Japan's surrender at the end of the Second World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-von-gneisenau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-von-gneisenau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August von Gneisenau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">August Neidhardt von Gneisenau was a Prussian field marshal and military reformer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-von-kotzebue"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-von-kotzebue.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">August von Kotzebue</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">August von Kotzebue was a German playwright and writer, one of the most performed dramatists of his time, who also served the Russian government.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusta-raurica"><span class="enc-name">Augusta Raurica</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusta Raurica is a Roman town site at Augst near Basel, Switzerland, on the Rhine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusta-vindelicorum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusta-vindelicorum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusta Vindelicorum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusta Vindelicorum was the Roman town that became Augsburg, which grew from a military base set up after the Roman conquest of the Alpine region in 15 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augusta-sicily"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augusta-sicily.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augusta, Sicily</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Augusta is a port town on the east coast of Sicily, between Catania and Syracuse, founded by the emperor Frederick II in 1232.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustal-prefect"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustal-prefect.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustal prefect</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Augustal prefect (praefectus augustalis) was the title of the governor of Egypt in the late Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auguste-comte"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auguste-comte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auguste Comte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Auguste Comte was a French philosopher of the nineteenth century and the founder of positivism, which emphasised science, order and progress.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auguste-de-marmont"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auguste-de-marmont.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auguste de Marmont</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Auguste de Marmont was a French general and Marshal of the Empire under Napoleon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/auguste-pavie"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/auguste-pavie.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Auguste Pavie</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Auguste Pavie was a French explorer and diplomat who mapped much of Laos and Cambodia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/augustin-calmet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/augustin-calmet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Augustin Calmet<